     "hidden": false,
     "text": "What was made and why: Percutaneous implants need a tight skin seal; bare -> Si-substituted hydroxyapatite nanorods on Ti shoul -> Ti implant surface carrying a hydroxyapatite nanor -> Hydrothermal growth of HA and Si-HA nanorod coatin"
    },
    {
     "step": 2,
     "role": "read",
     "nodes": [
      "o1"
     ],
     "depends_on": [
      1
     ],
     "hidden": false,
     "text": "Open the panels F1a, F1b [SEM] and compare (compare across conditions)"
    },
    {
     "step": 3,
     "role": "observation",
     "nodes": [
      "o1"
     ],
     "depends_on": [
      2
     ],
     "hidden": true,
     "text": "Observation: Top views show densely packed rods with hexagonal ends in both coatings; no visible difference in rod shape"
    },
    {
     "step": 4,
     "role": "claim",
     "nodes": [
      "n7"
     ],
     "depends_on": [
      3
     ],
     "hidden": true,
     "text": "Claim it establishes: Both coatings are arrays of hexagonal nanorods growing from the Ti; Si does not change the rod morphology"
    },
    {
     "step": 5,
     "role": "downstream",
     "nodes": [
      "n19",
      "n14",
      "n20",
      "n21"
     ],
     "depends_on": [
      4
     ],
     "hidden": true,
     "text": "Feeds: Nanorod topography raises protein adsorption, which with Si substitution promotes initial fibroblast"
    }
   ],
   "question": "Given the SEM top-view images in panels F1a and F1b (the bare HA-coated and the Si-HA-coated Ti surfaces), how does the nanorod morphology compare between the two coatings?",
   "answer_key": "Both top views show densely packed rods with hexagonal ends, and there is no visible difference in rod shape between the two coatings.",
